WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.490 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I'm Ken Duncan I'm the founder and chief  creative officer of Duncan animation  studio and I'm here to tell my story my  name is Stephanie Alexander I am a  storyboard revisionists and animator and  I'm here to tell my story  yeah I've been drawing since I was  really young I still have a comic book  that I did with a friend at the age of 8  we did a 50 page comic book we were  always drawing cartoons my sister told  me about this guy Walt Disney and I went  to the library and read about him and I  thought his life is so cool and he was  doing cartoons so from then on I wanted  to be an animator so I've always wanted  to draw I've been drawing since I was  really little and I got really serious  about drawing probably when I was around  10 years old I would buy pretty much  every how to draw book that I could find  in high school I started to find visual  development books for animated films and  that inspired me to go into animation as  a field actually one of my biggest  mentors was when I started to work in  the industry ended up in Europe and I  worked in London for an Oscar Grillo  Argentinean gentleman who had a studio  in London doing commercials and I learnt  a lot from him he was an amazing artist  and he owned his own studio so it's kind  of inspiring that he he could do both so  one of my earliest mentors in school was  David Brooks MA he was at the time and  elke had one of the co-chairs of my  major and he was xas knee he really  inspired me to develop my animation in  art style further my first job in  animation was during college our teacher  was being asked to be an animation  supervisor in a studio in Paris he was  looking for an assistant and a bunch of  us put in our portfolios and I was hired  to go to Paris and work on an animated  feature my first job coming out of  school was as a animation assistant on  Mary Poppins Returns and I got that job  actually through my mentor David Brooks  ma kin at Duncan studio reached out to  him first to come on as an animator and  when he couldn't he put forward several  students including myself to go in and  test for an animation assistant position  eventually I got the job so my career  really began in Europe I worked on  features I went to London to Madrid  working on commercials and ended up in  Ireland  working on a film called land before  time so for most of my career I've been  an immigrant for 35 years working in  Europe in the United States about a year  after coming to United States I ended up  at Disney in the 1990s I've been in this  industry now for two years officially my  first job out of college was Mary  Poppins returns as an animation  assistant and when that was done I was  eventually brought on at a studio called  oddbod Inc to work as a storyboard  revisionist on Muppet Babies where I am  currently located working with Ken and  the crew at Duncan Studio on Mary  Poppins was a really incredible  experience for me a lot of the staff a  lot of the people there were ecstasy and  were people that I had grown up watching  you know I would be talking with an  animator and he would just in passing be  like oh yeah I animated that frame of  the Iron Giant that you see right there  and I would just be like oh my god that  was one of the things that inspired me  to become an animator I feel I became  successful or made it as they say  probably when I became a supervisor at  Disney on a film called Hercules I did  the Meg character well thanks for  everything of work it's been a real  slice and then after that I did the Jane  character in Captain Elliott yeah  Treasure Planet and dr. again with the  greatest possible respect zip your  howling screamer so during that phase I  really felt they had made it as a  supervising animator I thought when I  had started on Poppins that that was  when I made it because it's like oh this  is my dream job this is what I've always  wanted to do and I quickly realized once  I was a few months in that you still  have to figure out what comes next you  have to figure out what comes after this  I think animation is an industry you  never quite feel like you make it  because there's always something new and  challenging around the corner for you  that's kind of what helps keep people in  a way humble for many decades animation  in general it was an unwritten rule that  sort of men were the animators and women  were the assistants or the cleanup  department I've talked to a couple of  women about this and they they truly  felt this  so first anybody to be an artist or an  animator you need some help some  assistance and some encouragement so  if there was a feeling that you weren't  getting encouraged I think it dissuaded  people from advancing I have  occasionally kind of been the butt of a  joke or two of like oh you're a pretty  good animator for a girl it's a small  offense it's not something that I really  take to heart but I do think that it's  kind of a symptom of a greater problem  we're challenging those old ideas about  where a woman or minorities place is in  this industry so I did work really hard  to get where I am I'm not really good at  what I do in spite of being woman I'm  really I'm just really good at what I  set out to do because I put that time  and effort into it my goals for the  future  for the studio really we're trying to  develop our own content and currently  working on a feature film idea that  really has a female lead and has an  ecological aspect to the story the  female doesn't need a male character to  help her we've also got a sci-fi series  etcetera so looking forward to creating  some really cool stuff my goals are  always changing as a storyboard  revisionists right now as somebody who  works in other people's boards I would  love to have a chance to do storyboards  myself and maybe even eventually get  into directing but there's definitely a  part of me that wants to go back into a  traditional animation field and into a  project that really brings attention  back to that art form which I really  admire about Duncan Studio and Ken's  work is that he does keep that alive and  I think that that's really admirable for  kids who really want to be an animator  it's really important to be curious and  to really research and to study people  study life study design and really reach  out to other people that have done the  art form to learn from them if you  pursue the art form that you love you're  going to do just fine  it's challenging but it's incredibly  rewarding you need to make sure that  you're putting the effort in putting the  time and you're really learning film  language and learning how to be a team  player learning how to  elaborate I would say this industry is  part luck part who you know and a big  part being prepared the relevant skills  a real portfolio when an opportunity  arrives because I've missed  opportunities because I wasn't ready and  I've gotten opportunities because I've  been ready in order for our animation  art form to advance we have to be more  inclusive to include people from  different cultures including more women  to get their point of view on stories  and to really show the world a new way  of looking at entertainment I think that  animation is wonderful it's it's a great  medium and it's unlimited and what you  can tell in animation and that's what I  really admire about it  [Music]

